course: biology
lesson: 252
title: Plants deeper: do trees talk? Fungi, warning smells and giant clones
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Trees cannot walk, shout or run away, but they are not as silent as they look. Under the forest floor, thin threads of fungi wrap around their roots, and in the air they give off smells that change when they are hurt.
explain: The fungus threads trade with the tree: the fungus brings water and minerals from far through the soil, and the tree pays it with sugar. Because one fungus can touch many trees, people call this the «Wood Wide Web».
explain: When you mow the lawn, the lovely smell of cut grass is really the grass sending out chemical signals because it has been hurt. Other plants, and even some insects, can pick such signals up.
explain: Scientists are still finding out how much trees really share through fungi. Some exciting claims are proven, others are still argued about, and good science says which is which.
fact: Many fungi in the soil join tree roots with fine threads; this partnership is called mycorrhiza, and about nine in ten kinds of plant have such fungus partners; the mushrooms that pop up in a ring round a tree often grow from these fungus threads.
fact: The fungus brings the tree water and minerals, and the tree pays the fungus with sugar made in its leaves.
fact: Fungus threads can join many trees, and some sugar and signals may pass from tree to tree, but how much and how often is still debated by scientists.
fact: The idea of a «mother tree» feeding her young through fungi comes from some scientists' work; it is a helpful picture, but trees do not feel love the way people do, and the evidence is argued about.
fact: When acacias are eaten, their leaves become more bitter, and they may give off a gas that makes nearby acacias turn bitter too; giraffes often move on and browse trees upwind.
fact: In South Africa in the 1980s many kudu antelopes died on crowded farms, and one explanation was that the acacias there had turned too bitter.
fact: The smell of cut grass is a chemical signal given off by the hurt grass; some plants chewed by caterpillars give off smells that call wasps which lay their eggs in caterpillars.
fact: Plants make bitter or poisonous chemicals after being attacked; a chewed tomato plant makes its leaves harder to digest.
fact: In an experiment, a small mustard-like plant made more defence chemicals after hearing a recording of caterpillars chewing; plants have no ears but can respond to vibrations.
fact: Some old tree stumps stay alive for years because their roots are joined to nearby trees, which keep them fed with sugar.
fact: A grove of aspens can be one giant plant, all trunks joined by one root system with the same genes; Pando in Utah weighs about 6,000 tonnes, and the trunks of such a clone can all turn gold on the same autumn day.
fact: A honey fungus in Oregon spreads under about 10 square kilometres of forest and may be thousands of years old, making it one of the largest living things.
fact: Old forests with big trees store huge amounts of carbon and are home to many fungi and creatures.
exercise: What is mycorrhiza? | A partnership of fungus threads and plant roots | A disease that kills the leaves of trees in summer | A kind of moss that grows on bark | A worm that lives deep inside the roots of old trees
exercise: Why do mushrooms often grow in a ring around a tree? | Their fungus threads are joined to the tree's roots | The tree drops its tiny mushroom seeds in a neat circle round it | Fairies dance there at night | Rain drips off the branches in a ring
exercise: What does the tree give its fungus partner? | Sugar made in its leaves | Fresh water from the rain | Its old dead leaves and nothing else | Nothing, the fungus steals everything
exercise: Why do people call the fungus links the «Wood Wide Web»? | One fungus can join many trees like a network | Trees send emails to each other | Spiders spin long webs between all the trees in the wood | The roots are made of wires
exercise: How sure are scientists about trees sharing food through fungi? | Some sharing happens, but how much is still debated | It is fully proven that all trees share equally | It has been proven completely false | Nobody has ever studied it at all
exercise: Do trees love their young the way the «mother tree» picture suggests? | No, it is a helpful picture, but trees do not feel love like people | Yes, trees cry real tears of sap when a young tree near them dies | Yes, they sing to their seedlings at night | No, old trees always poison young trees
exercise: What may happen to acacias when giraffes start eating them? | They make their leaves more bitter and may warn nearby trees | They grow longer and sharper thorns within a minute or two of the first bite | They drop all their leaves at once | They pull their branches away from the giraffe
exercise: What happened to many kudu in South Africa in the 1980s? | They died on crowded farms where acacias may have turned too bitter | They became much too fat from eating so many sweet acacia leaves every day | They moved to live with giraffes | They learned to eat grass instead
exercise: What is the smell of freshly cut grass? | A chemical signal given off by hurt grass | The smell of the mower's petrol only | The smell of worms in the soil | The smell of flowers opening
exercise: How can a plant chewed by caterpillars get help? | It gives off smells that call wasps which attack caterpillars | It shakes its leaves until they fall off | It shouts out so loudly that birds come from far away to help it | It walks away to a safer spot
exercise: How does a tomato plant defend itself after being chewed? | It makes its leaves harder to digest | It grows little legs and walks off somewhere else | It turns invisible to all the insects nearby | It drops all of its fruit at once on their heads
exercise: What did plants do after hearing a recording of caterpillars chewing? | They made more defence chemicals | They grew tiny ears on their leaves | They did nothing at all, as if deaf | They grew faster towards the sound
exercise: How can an old tree stump stay alive for years? | Its roots are joined to nearby trees that feed it | It eats the insects living in it | It makes its own food with no leaves at all, in the dark | It drinks extra rain through its rings
exercise: Why can a whole grove of aspens turn gold on the same day? | All the trunks can be one plant with the same genes | The wind tells them all at once | A farmer paints them together | They all saw the first frost together by chance
exercise: About how much does the aspen clone Pando weigh? | About 6,000 tonnes | About 6 tonnes, like an elephant | About 60 kilograms, like a man | About 6 million tonnes
exercise: Why is the honey fungus in Oregon so special? | It spreads under about 10 square kilometres of forest | It is the tallest mushroom anyone has ever seen, as high as a house | It glows bright enough to read by | It can walk slowly through the woods
exercise: Why are old forests with big trees special? | They store lots of carbon and are home to many living things | They have no fungi, insects or birds living in them at all any more | They make no oxygen any more | They are all made of one single tree
test: «trees literally text each other through mushrooms, saw it on TikTok» What is the fairest answer? | Fungus threads link trees and may pass sugar and signals, but how much is still debated | Yes, trees send real words to each other along their roots, just like phone calls to friends | No, fungi and trees never touch at all | Yes, and they use the forest's wifi
test: Count them: 4 giraffes are browsing one acacia. How many legs and how many necks? | 16 legs and 4 necks | 8 legs and 4 necks | 16 legs and 16 necks | 4 legs and 4 necks
test: A giraffe eats from one acacia, then walks a long way upwind to the next. Why might that be clever? | Trees upwind may not have smelled the warning gas yet | Upwind trees are always taller | The wind blows the leaves straight into its mouth so it eats more | Giraffes like the wind on their face
test: What would happen to many trees if all the fungi in the soil suddenly vanished? | They would struggle to get enough water and minerals | They would grow twice as fast | Nothing at all would change for them | They would start to eat insects instead
test: In a cartoon, the trees of a forest grab Tom with their branches when he chases Jerry. What is true of real trees? | Real trees cannot grab, but they can change their chemicals when attacked | Real trees can grab the animals that run past and hold them tight with branches | Real trees can walk slowly to catch cats | Real trees have no way of defending themselves from animals
test: «a whole hill of aspens and you're telling me it's one tree?? no way» What is right? | Yes, the trunks can share one root system and the same genes | No, every trunk is always a separate plant, grown from its own seed | No, aspens only grow alone | Yes, but only in fairy tales
test: «my nan's book says the mother tree loves her babies and feeds them sugar. true or made up?» What is best? | Some scientists think big trees pass sugar to young ones, but «love» is a human word | All true, trees love their babies and feel sad for them just like people do | All made up, trees never share anything | True, and the babies thank her too
test: You mow the lawn and smell the grass. What is going on? | Hurt grass is giving off chemical signals | The grass is making a sweet perfume for people | The hot mower blades are cooking the grass | Worms under the lawn are breathing out the smell
test: «how can the biggest living thing be a fungus if I can't even see it?» What is the answer? | Most of it is threads hidden under the ground | It moves far too fast for anyone to see it | It only comes out on dark nights when nobody is about | It is see-through like glass
test: A scientist plays a recording of wind to one plant and of caterpillars chewing to another. Which most likely makes more defence chemicals? | The one that heard the chewing | The one that heard the wind blowing | Both make exactly the same amount | Neither, plants cannot respond to vibrations
test: «found a stump in the woods with green stuff growing round the cut, but there's no leaves. how?» What is likely? | Its roots may be joined to nearby trees that feed it | It is eating the moss growing on its top | It makes food from the dark soil | It is a fake stump made of plastic
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the association between soil fungi and the roots of trees. | The fungus supplies water and minerals and receives sugar in return | The fungus is a parasite which invariably kills the tree within a few years | The tree supplies water and receives sugar in return | No exchange of any substance occurs between them
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present state of scientific knowledge concerning the transfer of sugar between trees via fungi? | Some transfer is observed, but its extent and importance remain under debate | It has been conclusively proven that all trees share equally with their neighbours | It has been conclusively disproven in all forests | It has never been the subject of investigation
exam: Identify the response of an acacia tree pursuant to its being browsed by herbivores. | It renders its foliage more bitter and may emit a gas detected by neighbouring trees | It abscises all foliage forthwith | It produces additional sweet nectar to placate the herbivore and so avert further damage | It undergoes no alteration whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a stand of aspen trees may be regarded as a single organism. | The trunks arise from a single connected root system and share identical genes | The trunks were planted on the same day by a single forester, from one bag of seed | The trunks are joined by their branches above ground | The trunks are all of identical height
